<h2 id="code-walkthrough">Annotated Code Walkthrough — SSE Streaming Endpoint</h2>

<p>This walkthrough traces the SSE streaming mechanism from the route handler through the event generator, showing how each layer contributes to the real-time experience.</p>

<div class="code-walk">
  <div class="step">
    <strong>Route receives the request.</strong> The route handler validates the incoming request body using a Pydantic model, extracting <code>flow_id</code>, <code>tickers</code>, and <code>date_range</code>. FastAPI automatically returns 422 if validation fails.
    <pre><code class="language-python">@router.post("/api/hedge-fund/run")
async def run_hedge_fund_endpoint(
    request: RunRequest,
    graph_service: GraphService = Depends(),
    agent_service: AgentService = Depends(),
):
    workflow = graph_service.compile(request.flow_id)
    event_gen = agent_service.execute(workflow, request.tickers, request.date_range)
    return EventSourceResponse(event_gen)</code></pre>
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <strong>GraphService compiles the flow.</strong> The service loads the flow's JSON graph from the database, validates it as a DAG, runs topological sort, and resolves each node's model configuration. The result is a <code>CompiledWorkflow</code> — a flat, ordered list of agent configs ready for sequential execution.
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <strong>AgentService creates an async generator.</strong> The <code>execute</code> method is an <code>async def</code> generator function. It first yields a <code>StartEvent</code>, then iterates over agents, yielding a <code>ProgressUpdateEvent</code> after each one completes.
    <pre><code class="language-python">async def execute(self, workflow, tickers, date_range):
    run_id = str(uuid4())
    yield StartEvent(run_id=run_id, agents=[a.name for a in workflow.agents])

    results = []
    for i, agent_config in enumerate(workflow.agents):
        try:
            result = await self._run_agent(agent_config, tickers, date_range)
            results.append(result)
            self.repo.save_cycle(run_id, agent_config.name, result)
            yield ProgressUpdateEvent(
                agent=agent_config.name,
                progress=(i + 1) / len(workflow.agents),
                data=result.summary
            )
        except Exception as e:
            yield ErrorEvent(agent=agent_config.name, error=str(e))

    portfolio = self._aggregate(results)
    self.repo.save_run(run_id, portfolio, status="completed")
    yield CompleteEvent(portfolio=portfolio, run_id=run_id)</code></pre>
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <strong>FastAPI wraps the generator in EventSourceResponse.</strong> Each yielded event is serialized to JSON and formatted as an SSE message: <code>data: {json}\n\n</code>. The response uses <code>Content-Type: text/event-stream</code> and keeps the connection alive with periodic heartbeat comments.
  </div>

  <div class="step">
    <strong>Browser receives events via EventSource.</strong> The React frontend opens an <code>EventSource</code> connection. Each incoming event is parsed and dispatched to the appropriate handler — updating the progress bar, showing agent results, or rendering final portfolio allocations.
    <pre><code class="language-javascript">const source = new EventSource(`/api/hedge-fund/run/${runId}/stream`);
source.onmessage = (event) => {
  const data = JSON.parse(event.data);
  switch (data.type) {
    case 'start':    setAgents(data.agents); break;
    case 'progress': updateProgress(data.agent, data.progress); break;
    case 'error':    showError(data.agent, data.error); break;
    case 'complete': showResults(data.portfolio); source.close(); break;
  }
};</code></pre>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<h2 id="design-rationale">Design Rationale</h2>

<h3 id="rationale-service-layer">Service Layer Separation</h3>

<p>Routes are intentionally thin — they validate input, call a service, and return the response. All business logic lives in dedicated service classes. This separation provides three key benefits: (1) services can be unit-tested without spinning up a web server, (2) the same service can be used by multiple routes (e.g., <code>AgentService</code> serves both the run and model-listing endpoints), and (3) swapping the web framework (FastAPI → Flask) would only require rewriting the route layer, not the business logic.</p>

<h3 id="rationale-repository">Repository Pattern</h3>

<p>Services never call SQLAlchemy directly. Instead, they depend on repository interfaces that abstract away database operations. This indirection means tests can inject mock repositories (returning pre-built objects without touching SQLite), and the data layer can be swapped from SQLite to PostgreSQL by providing a new repository implementation — without changing a single line of service code.</p>

<h3 id="rationale-sse">SSE Over WebSockets</h3>

<p>The communication pattern between server and client during a run is strictly unidirectional: the server pushes progress updates to the client. WebSockets would be overkill — they add connection upgrade complexity, require explicit heartbeat management, and don't auto-reconnect. SSE provides all of this out of the box: automatic reconnection with <code>Last-Event-ID</code>, standard HTTP transport (works through proxies and CDNs), and native browser support via the <code>EventSource</code> API. The only trade-off is the one-way limitation, which perfectly matches this use case.</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<h2 id="deep-dive-cards">Deep-Dive Topics</h2>

<div class="card-grid">
  <div class="card">
    <h4>SSE Event System</h4>
    <p>The event hierarchy is built on Pydantic models. <code>BaseEvent</code> provides <code>type</code>, <code>timestamp</code>, and <code>sequence</code> fields. Subclasses (<code>StartEvent</code>, <code>ProgressUpdateEvent</code>, <code>ErrorEvent</code>, <code>CompleteEvent</code>) add domain-specific fields. The <code>type</code> field acts as a discriminator for frontend event routing. Sequence numbers enable gap detection on reconnect — if the client sees sequence 5 after sequence 3, it knows it missed an event.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h4>Database Schema</h4>
    <p>Four tables form the data model. <code>hedge_fund_flows</code> stores graph definitions. <code>hedge_fund_flow_runs</code> records execution metadata (status, timestamps, parameters). <code>hedge_fund_flow_run_cycles</code> captures per-agent output within a run. <code>api_keys</code> holds encrypted provider credentials. All tables use UUID primary keys and include <code>created_at</code> / <code>updated_at</code> timestamps managed by SQLAlchemy event hooks.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="card">
    <h4>Graph Service Compilation</h4>
    <p>The <code>GraphService</code> converts a React Flow graph (JSON with <code>nodes</code> and <code>edges</code> arrays) into an executable workflow via three passes: (1) DAG validation — cycle detection using DFS with coloring, (2) topological sort — Kahn's algorithm producing a linear execution order, and (3) config resolution — merging node-level model selections with global defaults. Invalid graphs return 422 errors with node-specific messages.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<h2 id="database-models">Database Models</h2>

<table>
  <thead>
